<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; border: 2px solid #a7d9ed;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; padding-bottom: 5px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # This Is Me! – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin: 0;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Pre-K (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Family</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> This Is Me!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Reading: Identify characters in a picture book. Phonemic Awareness: Identify environmental sounds and speech sounds.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can point to family members in a picture book (e.g., pointing to the picture of the father as the teacher reads a book to the class). Can match images of family members with words (e.g., matching the word "mom" or "dad" with their corresponding picture). Can identify the initial speech sound in familiar spoken words, supported by pictures (e.g., pointing to the picture of "mom" when hearing the /m/ sound or "sister" when hearing the /s/ sound).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. This stage activates prior knowledge and sets the context for reading about family members.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads the class in singing "Finger Family Song" using hand puppets or finger representations for "Mommy finger, Daddy finger, Brother finger, Sister finger, Baby finger." Students point to their fingers as they sing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up large flashcards of "mom," "dad," "brother," "sister," "baby," and "family." Teacher points to each picture, saying the word clearly, e.g., "This is mom. Mom." Then the teacher models identifying initial sounds: "Mom starts with /m/, /m/, /m/." and "Sister starts with /s/, /s/, /s/." The teacher demonstrates pointing to the correct flashcard when hearing the word or its initial sound.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher shows the flashcards again and asks:
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to 'mom' flashcard) "Is this mom? Yes or no?" (Students respond with gestures or choral 'yes'/'no').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to 'dad' flashcard) "Who is this?" (Students respond with 'dad' or point to their own dad).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Family." Focus on reading comprehension through a picture book.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple, brightly illustrated picture book titled "My Happy Family" featuring a family with a mom, dad, brother, sister, baby, and pet (dog/cat). The book should have clear, repetitive text (e.g., "This is my mom. She is happy." "This is my brother. He likes to play.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying characters in a picture book and understanding simple descriptive sentences related to them.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher reads the picture book aloud, pointing to the characters and words.
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> While reading, the teacher pauses and asks, "Who is this?" (pointing to a character). Students point to the character in the book and/or say the name (e.g., "mom," "dad").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (pointing):</b> "Is this the baby?" (pointing to the sister). Students shake head or give a thumbs down for 'no', nod or thumbs up for 'yes', while the teacher corrects and points to the correct character.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching (visual):</b> "Point to the dog in the book!" (Students follow the instruction). "Point to the happy mom."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading. Students practice identifying family members and initial sounds in words, supported by visuals.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Naming family members (mom, dad, brother, sister, baby, family), using "Who is it? This is my..." and identifying initial speech sounds /m/ and /s/.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Picture-Word Match:</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with pictures of family members (mom, dad, brother, sister, baby) on one side and the corresponding words on the other. Students use crayons to draw lines matching each picture to its word, guided by the teacher who reads each word aloud. For Pre-K, these "words" can be simple, distinct labels, possibly with a slightly larger first letter to aid recognition.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled - Sound & Find:</b> The teacher calls out initial sounds or words. For example, "Can you find someone that starts with /m/?" (Students point to 'mom' on their worksheet or a large flashcard). "Can you find someone that starts with /s/?" (Students point to 'sister'). The teacher also says full words: "Point to 'dad'!" or "Point to 'baby'!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction/Peer Check - "Read and Point":</b> Students work in pairs with a set of family flashcards or their completed worksheets. One student picks a card/points to a picture and asks, "Who is it?" The other student points to the correct picture and says the name (e.g., "Mom!"). They take turns, focusing on visually identifying and naming.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Family." Focus on fluency and communicative task completion.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Family Picture Book Creation" – Each student receives a pre-drawn outline of a simple house and stick figures representing family members. They color or paste pictures of family members (mom, dad, brother, sister, baby) they have cut out or previously drawn. The teacher helps them label their chosen family members with simple words like "mom" or "dad" by writing the word clearly next to the drawing. Students then 'read' their individual "My Family" page to a small group or the teacher.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive materials and color/paste their family members into the house outline.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher circulates, assisting with labeling (writing the word "mom" next to their mom's drawing, etc.) and asking "Who is this?" to prompt identification.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students sit in a circle. Each student takes a turn holding up their "My Family" page. The teacher points to a family member on their page and asks, "Who is this?" The student responds by pointing and saying, "My mom" or "My dad" or "This is my sister." This is a guided reading of their own creation.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment to check students' reading comprehension and phonemic awareness of family vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on individual student responses during the "My Family Picture Book Creation" and a quick "Find and Point" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows three large flashcards: 'mom', 'sister', 'baby'. The teacher asks individual students, "Point to mom." "Point to the picture that starts with /s/." "Who is this?" (pointing to baby).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students hold up their completed "My Family" pages. The teacher walks around and individually asks, "Who is this?" pointing to a family member they drew/pasted, assessing their ability to identify and name.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly points to family members (mom, dad, brother, sister, baby) in pictures when named by the teacher.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies initial speech sounds (/m/ in "mom", /s/ in "sister") by pointing to the correct picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to name family members (e.g., "mom," "dad") in response to "Who is this?".</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are shown two large cards: a happy face and a sad face. "Can you point to mom and dad in a book?" If yes, students hold up the happy face. If no, the sad face. "Did you like learning about family?" (thumbs up/down).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reflecting on the lesson's effectiveness and planning for future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite picture in the family book today?" (Students point or try to say the name).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who did you learn about today?" (Students name a family member).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The picture book reading and matching activities worked well for visual learners in Pre-K. Students were engaged with identifying characters. Some students still need more practice with distinguishing initial sounds /m/ and /s/, so further reinforcement will be needed in the next lesson. The physical movement and pointing during activities were very effective. I might add more TPR for initial sounds next time. The pairing activity was a bit challenging for some, requiring more direct teacher modeling first. Need to simplify the instructions for pair work even further for this age group.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on reading and identifying. The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will build on this vocabulary by having students actively speak and interact about their own families, using the same family member vocabulary and simple questions like "Who is this?".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>